  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia has helped Indonesia boost its rice productivity by partnering with 5 national rice seed companies to produce and promote high-yielding variety (HYV) seeds. HYV seeds are a good choice to combat food insecurity, since it has the potential to increase productivity by at least 30%. As of December 2022, 16,346 smallholder farming households has benefited from better access to these seeds that are also drought, pest and disease tolerant. Think tanks were engaged to advocate Ministry of Agriculture and other relevant government stakeholders to boost the distribution of HYV seeds in its programs.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Chemical fertiliser from Indonesia makes up 1.9% of global agriculture emissions.  The overuse of nitrogen-based fertiliser also led to poor soil quality in many intensively farmed areas. During this period, Australia continued its partnership with fertiliser companies to promote the balanced use of fertiliser, including organic and mineral-based fertiliser, which would minimise the risk of chemical runoff and pollution, provide plants with essential nutrients and give smallholder farmers with a productivity boost through improved soil quality. 
&#x200d;
Australia&#x2019;s partners expanded their organic and mineral fertiliser product distribution to new locations and adapted their marketing strategy by educating farmers on good fertilisation practices. The efficient use of nitrogen-based fertiliser has also reduced emissions of 6,860 tonnes of carbon dioxide equivalent (CO2e). By adopting appropriate fertiliser applications, 88,931 men and 28,609 women farmers increased their productivity and reduced farming costs, which led to increased income by 44%.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">During this reporting period, Australia continued to work with rice seed producers to promote high-yielding rice seed varieties to smallholder farmers. One of the partners, PT Botani Seed Indonesia, developed a variety requiring intermittent irrigation, making it adaptable for diverse environments. This variety is also more resistant to pest and disease, suitable for mechanised harvesting, and reduced harvest loss, resulting in higher productivity and cost reduction especially for farmers in drought area. Australia supported the company with business strategy development, which led to PT Botani expanding their production capacity from 2 tons to 80 tons, with the aim of scaling to 500 tons in the next two years. 
&#x200d;
Overall, rice seed partners increased their production capacity and continued promoting quality rice seed to farmers. Adoption of high yielding variety seed by rice farmers resulted in a 31% income increase for 40,011 men and 24,721 women farmers.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
